# Approval before implementation

Do **not** edit files, run destructive commands, or ship features until the user explicitly approves the approach.

## Required workflow

1. **Understand** — Read relevant code and any design references the user provides.
2. **Clarify** — If scope, UX, architecture, or acceptance criteria are unclear, ask questions **before** planning or coding. Label questions **Q1**, **Q2**, **Q3**, etc. so the user can answer with matching keys.
3. **Propose** — Share a short plan: what will change, which files, and any trade-offs. For larger work, use a concise plan (bullets or checklist), not a surprise diff.
4. **Wait** — Proceed only after explicit approval (e.g. “go ahead”, “implement it”, “approved”, “yes”).
5. **Implement** — Keep changes scoped to what was approved.

## When to ask questions

Ask when any of these are unknown or ambiguous:

- Feature scope (what is in / out of v1)
- UI/UX (layout, copy, navigation) especially when a Figma or mock exists
- Data model (local vs API, fields, persistence)
- Breaking changes or migrations
- Multiple valid implementations with different trade-offs

Do **not** block on trivial details you can infer from existing project patterns.

## Exceptions (still prefer a one-line heads-up)

- User message is clearly “fix this bug now” on a specific file/error they pasted
- User says “just do it” / “implement” after you already presented a plan in the same thread
- Tiny follow-ups clearly in scope of an **already approved** task (e.g. “make the toast 1s longer” after shopping list was approved)

## After implementation

Summarize what changed and where. Do not start unrelated refactors unless approved.
